  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 1217 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nHeavy rainfall potential still the main concern for West Central \nTexas for Tuesday Night through at least Thursday. The question \nreally is where and how much and those answers are not a lot clearer \ntonight than they were yesterday.\n\nEnsembles from the various models continue to be clustered in 2 \nscenarios, one from the GFS and many of its members that tends to \nhave more of an open upper level trough that is a little farther \nnortheast, and the ensembles favored by the other global models that \nforecast more of a slower, closed off upper low, that is a little \nfarther southwest. (Part of these difference is just how the \ndifferent models ingest and incorporate the remnants of Polo in the \ninitial upper trough.) The difference between these 2 setups in how \nfar east the dry slot spreads and thus where the heaviest band of \nrainfall will develop. In either setup though, the eastern counties \nincluding the Heartland and Hill Country see heavy rainfall. Its \nreally matter of whether the heavier rain sets up farther west and \nincludes Abilene, San Angelo, and more of the Big Country, Concho \nValley, and Edwards Plateau or if the dry slot shifts farther east \nand limits the rainfall across the western half of the area. \n\nAs for totals, the average precip from the LREF continues to be \nmainly in the 2-3 inch range for almost all of West Central Texas, \nand the 90th percentile has crept up into the 4-5 inch across most \nareas. Totals for the 95% percentile are in the 5-7 range with even \nhigher Max totals in some areas (10+). With some of the model PWATS \nstill near seasonal maxes, these higher numbers can not be completely \nruled out if the worst case situation ends up working out and the dry \nslot does not shift across the area. Conversely, low end totals\nreflect the possible dry slot and haven't budged much off the\n1/3 to 2/3 inch range either, so uncertainty remains abundant. \nWPC has already placed much of the area in Moderate for Excessive \nRainfall for Day 3 and 4 and this seems completely reasonable for \nnow.\n\nJust now starting to see some of the CAMs and high-res model data \nget out that far (RRFS) and more will follow over the next 24 hours. \nThis will provide a little better look at any possible rainfall \ntotals and we can start to narrow down the possible QPF totals and \nwhere the heaviest totals may be.\n\nOtherwise, temperatures haven't changed much and not going to argue \nwith the blends at this point. Well above normal temperatures  for \nTuesday, with much cooler conditions for late week into the weekend.",
